WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.622 --> 00:00:11.705
(audience murmuring)

00:00:17.855 --> 00:00:19.434
(sharp bang)

00:00:19.434 --> 00:00:20.267
- Ah.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.810
So good afternoon, thank
you all for turning out

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:36.660
on this very sad, dreary weather.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.450
And my apologies to anyone who
was rained on exceptionally

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:43.310
hard as you made the
approach to Kroon Hall.

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:45.320
But this is such a lovely building.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.670
The warm wood I hope everyone
will warm up very quickly

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.933
as we begin today's program.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:54.920
It gives me great pleasure to welcome all

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.450
of you to this fall semester's
Chubb Fellowship Lecture

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:02.490
with our distinguished guest
ambassador Joao Vale de Almeida

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:05.070
the head of the European Union delegation

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.270
to the United Nations.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:09.650
Now before we begin I
just wanna ask everyone

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:12.600
to turn off all cell phones
and refrain from taking

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.690
any photographs with the exception

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:17.070
of our staff photographer.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:19.210
This way we will make
sure that the program

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.363
will not be interrupted this afternoon.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.220
And for those of you who
do not know who I am,

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.090
my name is Mary Lui.

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:28.250
I'm a professor in the
department of history

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.640
and the program in American studies.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.673
I'm also the current head
of Timothy Dwight College.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.980
As the head of Timothy
Dwight College I have

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:39.030
the honor of serving as the custodian

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:42.210
of the illustrious Chubb
Fellowship established

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:45.450
in 1941 out of a prior large donation

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.950
for education purposes.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.300
This was made in 1936 by
Hendon Chubb who graduated

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.060
from the Yale class of 1895.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.380
Since establishment the
Chubb fund has adhered

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:58.320
to the goals of providing encouragement

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.020
and aid to students
interested in government

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.843
and American public affairs.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.330
The fellowship initially
aimed to foster among Yale

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.890
undergraduates an
interest in public service

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:10.810
and local and state affairs.

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:14.180
And it grew over the years to
include numerous distinguished

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:17.100
visitors and national
and international affairs

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.060
as well as leaders in the world
of the arts and humanities.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.830
Since the 1940s the Chubb
fellows lecture series

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.270
has inspired generations of Yale students

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:30.030
to undertake public service
and pursue leadership roles

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.197
in the hopes of creating
a better world today

00:02:32.197 --> 00:02:33.923
and for posterity.

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:38.090
The fellowship has hosted
four US presidents,

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:43.060
George W. Bush, Gerald Ford,
Jimmy Carter, and Harry Truman.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.270
In recent years we have
welcomed a wide range

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:47.280
of national and international leaders

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.550
including Wynton Marsalis,
Aung San Suu Kyi,

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:51.833
and Shah Rukh Khan.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:54.730
But this quick description of the history

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:57.500
and aims of the Chubb
Fellowship I'm delighted

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:00.690
that we're welcoming to
Yale today ambassador

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:01.853
João Vale de Almeida.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.680
Now introducing the ambassador

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:08.870
will be professor James
Levinsohn who is a TD fellow.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:12.150
He's the Charles W. Goodyear
professor in global affairs

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.640
at Yale's Jackson Institute
for Global affairs

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.900
and professor of economics and management

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.480
at the Yale School of Management.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.530
Professor Levinsohn is
the founding director

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:24.230
of the Jackson Institute
and in that capacity he

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:27.630
oversees the global affairs
major in Yale College

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:32.570
the global affairs MA and the
Yale World Fellows program.

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:35.320
He's a member of the
Council on Foreign Relations

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:38.040
and is a research associate
of the National Bureau

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.970
of Economic Research.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:42.250
His current academic research is focused

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:45.480
on estimation of the impacts
of internal migration

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.980
on household well being in South Africa,

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:52.020
and estimating the demand
for sanitation in Bangladesh.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.630
So please join me in
welcoming professor Levinsohn

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:57.030
to the podium who will
give his introduction

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:58.372
of the ambassador.

00:03:58.372 --> 00:04:00.622
(applause)

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:05.780
- Thank you, Mary.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:10.780
Its an honor to be able to
introduce João Vale de Almeida.

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:14.650
Ambassador Vale de Almeida

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:17.860
is a senior European Union diplomat.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:21.800
He was appointed as EU
ambassador to the United Nations

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.350
in October 2015.

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:28.120
Prior to leading the
EU delegation to the UN

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:31.360
the ambassador served as
the first EU ambassador

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.160
to the United States.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:36.310
During this period he was
critical to the launching

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:39.150
of the trans Atlantic trade
and investment partnership

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:42.070
an ambitious free trade agreement that is,

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:45.370
or perhaps was being negotiated.

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:50.090
From 2004 to 2009
ambassador Vale de Almeida

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:54.313
was chief of staff for EU
president Jose Manuel Barroso.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.200
The ambassador is originally from Portugal

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:02.040
which is why I turned to our
own professor Nuno Monteiro

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.170
when I wondered how the
ambassador's last name

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:05.600
should be pronounced.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.953
Nuno bears no responsibility
for my mangling.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:14.340
We are fortunate to have
ambassador Vale de Almeida today

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:18.480
as there are so many
pressing EU related issues.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.470
We are witnessing the largest migration

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:25.000
in Europe since the close
of the second World War

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.080
and from where I sit this seems

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:31.500
to be putting existential
stresses on the EU.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:34.440
We've witnessed Brexit,
our own election here

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:37.680
about three weeks ago,
and several key elections

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.660
are forthcoming on the continent.

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:42.760
The future of the Paris Climate Agreement

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:46.470
is unclear and the US position
on many other important

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:50.610
international issues moving
forward is uncertain.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:54.140
In this environment its
a special opportunity

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:58.580
to hear from the EU's
ambassador to the UN.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:02.713
Please join me in welcoming
ambassador João Vale de Almeida.

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:05.879
(applause)

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:15.640
- Good, good evening.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:18.383
Thank you for coming today
in spite of the weather.

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:22.913
Its a great honor and
great pleasure to be here.

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:25.760
My name is João Vale de Almeida

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:28.090
which is a slightly
different pronunciation.

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:30.030
(laughing)

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:35.030
But you know I've been long
enough time in this job

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.013
to accept creativity.

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:41.960
And when my kids call
me or my wife calls me

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:46.700
it sounds a bit strange because
its the real pronunciation

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:50.826
but it shows the diversity
in which we live in,

00:06:50.826 --> 00:06:54.830
and live with and its good.

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:58.250
Its good to be called my name
in different pronunciations

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:01.460
as it is to be able to engage
with the different people

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:02.740
around the world.

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:07.740
As it is to discuss and be
faced with different opinions.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:10.100
This is what democracy is.

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:12.640
This is what globalization is.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:16.080
This is what a pluralistic society is.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:20.340
And its always good to be able to discuss

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.670
with people like you, faculty and students

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:25.063
and people interested in global affairs.

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:28.190
Its also good to come out of New York

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:30.200
and to come out of the United Nations

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:34.360
and being such a beautiful and
environmentally sustainable

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:37.430
building and be with such nice people

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:39.330
and I just had a wonderful conversation

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:42.220
with a number of members
of the faculty here.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:44.980
So I'd like to thank all
of you for coming tonight.

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:47.060
I would like to thank the professor for--

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:49.403
Sorry, introducing me.

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:53.760
And I hope didn't destroy
the whole operation here.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:55.433
Is this the right one, yes.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:59.590
Thank you professor Mary
Lui for organizing this.

00:07:59.590 --> 00:08:02.290
My good friend Mary Evelyn Tucker

00:08:02.290 --> 00:08:04.520
for being at the heart of the beginning

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:06.410
of all this operation.

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:07.780
Thank you, thank you all.

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:10.930
Thank you the school
for hosting us as well.

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:13.340
Its again a real pleasure, a real honor

00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:16.790
to be able to be here to discuss with you.

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:21.110
What can do to help you understand better

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:22.983
the way the world goes these days?

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:27.580
I think that will be my initial purpose

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:31.170
and I will be, I'm absolutely confident

00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:36.170
that I can describe the world
as it is today in 20 minutes

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:37.900
which will leave plenty of time for you

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:40.190
to question everything I would have said

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:42.860
and prove me wrong on all my assertions.

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:47.187
But let me try to do that in
a sort of telegraphic way.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:53.440
So and using where I am
today at the United Nations

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:56.810
but what I did before that as a basis

00:08:56.810 --> 00:09:01.170
and a background for my
view of the world today.

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:03.950
I think if you listen to the
debate you have basically

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:05.900
two ways of looking at the world today.

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:09.100
Two sort of views about the world.

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:13.543
One is a benign one and one
is a slightly darker one.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:18.360
The benign one basically
says that the world

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:20.153
has never been in a better shape.

00:09:23.290 --> 00:09:28.290
You take peace, you take
prosperity, you take democracy,

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:30.600
you take respect for human rights

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:36.430
and those who support
these ideas say that we

00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:41.330
have never seen such
level of accomplishment

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:42.433
in all these areas.

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:48.093
If you take life expectancy
around the world,

00:09:49.070 --> 00:09:54.070
we never had such promising
prospects for our lives.

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:56.690
Certainly in developed countries

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:59.470
but also increasingly so
in developing countries.

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:02.503
Child mortality has
gone down tremendously.

00:10:03.890 --> 00:10:07.223
Access to knowledge and
information and education.

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:13.400
Technological developments,
global solutions

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:14.643
for global problems.

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:18.780
And we are in an
environmental school here.

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:20.860
What have we achieved
with the Paris Agreement

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:23.363
is unheard of in the past.

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:26.170
The Cold War is over.

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:29.890
The number of wars around
the world is much less

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:33.363
important than it was decades ago.

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:37.560
We lifted millions, hundreds of millions

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:41.963
of people out of poverty
in the last few decades.

00:10:43.188 --> 00:10:45.003
There is a new global middle class.

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:51.463
And everyday new people
add to this middle class.

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:55.010
And terrorism is there, of course,

00:10:55.010 --> 00:10:58.250
but if you look at the
figures and I came across

00:10:58.250 --> 00:11:02.880
an interesting figure
of this benign school

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:04.900
of thought about the
situation in the world

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:07.960
which tells me that the
victims of terrorism

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:11.160
in America are less
numerous than the victims

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:13.927
of falling furniture in the US homes.

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:22.230
So this is some element of a benign view

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:23.130
of the world today.

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:25.120
There is a darker view of the world

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:29.230
and that view of the world tells us

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:32.773
that basically the world
is on the brink of chaos.

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:35.623
There's no one in charge.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:41.780
Terrorists, refugees, migrants,
dominate the landscape.

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:44.913
And no one cares about it
and no one controls that.

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:50.483
The West is in decline and
the rest is not to be trusted.

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:57.960
Politicians, businessmen
are basically corrupt.

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:03.110
We cannot trust them and
we have to, we people

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:07.820
have to take care of things somehow.

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:10.060
Our leaders are by definition weak.

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:13.183
Weaker than previous leaders.

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:16.953
Terror is everywhere.

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:20.560
Trade is bad for jobs.

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:23.023
The only solution is
close down our borders.

00:12:25.150 --> 00:12:26.970
This is a darker vision of the world

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:31.760
that we also witness and that is present

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:32.893
in the public debate.

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:39.223
Of course none of these two
views are entirely accurate.

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:44.970
None of these descriptions of the world

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:48.633
is the right one.

00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:53.813
But there are elements of truth in both.

00:12:57.750 --> 00:13:01.770
Both of them describe
parts of today's reality

00:13:01.770 --> 00:13:02.603
in the world.

00:13:04.870 --> 00:13:09.870
But they also translate
or reflect not necessarily

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:13.513
the reality but the perception of reality.

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:16.093
That people have.

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:18.950
That public opinion have.

00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:25.150
And as someone said before me, politics

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:26.763
is a lot about perception.

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:32.950
Decisions are very often
and I would say increasingly

00:13:32.950 --> 00:13:37.383
so, made upon perception.

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:44.240
Emotions, feelings, rather
than necessarily facts

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:49.573
assessed against reality.

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:54.320
So what can we do, what
should we do having

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:58.843
as a background these two
opposite views of the world?

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:04.530
Having in mind this
intersection between reality

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:06.840
and perception of reality.

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:09.530
How should we perceive ourselves

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:12.330
in trying to understand better
where the world is today?

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:16.980
Let me try to help you in a modest way

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:19.030
to try to understand where we are.

00:14:19.030 --> 00:14:22.600
And I'll take three elements of analysis

00:14:23.750 --> 00:14:26.233
which I believe are close to facts.

00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:29.010
There's a lot of discussion today about

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:31.830
what facts are and what truth is.

00:14:31.830 --> 00:14:34.330
I won't get into that,
I'll leave it to you

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:37.703
but this is my view of the
facts, lets put it this way.

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:43.520
The first one is that I think we are

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:45.233
at the end of a cycle.

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:51.630
What characterizes cycle that in my view

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:55.070
is closing down as we speak.

00:14:55.070 --> 00:15:00.070
These are 25, 30 years
since basically around

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:01.843
the fall of Berlin wall.

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:07.530
And these years, these two and
a half decades, three decades

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:11.280
have been extremely rich, extremely rich.

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:12.623
Extremely fast.

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:18.880
This is the period of
what some economists call

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:21.240
the hyper globalization.

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:24.913
Where basically trade was
growing faster than GDP.

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:31.320
Where China came out of,
came out, came of age.

00:15:33.750 --> 00:15:37.023
And grew at basically two digits per year.

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:42.480
This is a time of an accelerated
technological revolution

00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:46.690
that we are still witnessing today.

00:15:46.690 --> 00:15:48.980
Which has created the
basis for the next one,

00:15:48.980 --> 00:15:51.840
for the so called fourth
industrial revolution

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:54.380
that is about to happen.

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:58.270
This is the period of
trade liberalization.

00:15:58.270 --> 00:16:03.080
This is a period of
deregulation to some extent.

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:04.750
In some areas more than others.

00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:09.750
This is a period of good
growth of the world economy.

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:14.593
Its also a period of increased mobility.

00:16:15.580 --> 00:16:18.650
Exponential growth of mobility.

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:23.433
Of people, of goods,
of services, capitals.

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:25.793
Never seen before.

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:30.460
This is the period where
social media emerged

00:16:30.460 --> 00:16:33.013
where connectivity developed.

00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:38.300
All this happened in a
short period of time.

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:42.713
Historically this is
two minutes in history.

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:45.850
I like to say that never so much happened

00:16:45.850 --> 00:16:47.860
that affected so many people in such

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:49.740
a short period of time.

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:51.950
This is a bad quote from Churchill,

00:16:51.950 --> 00:16:54.170
and adaptive quote from Churchill.

00:16:54.170 --> 00:16:56.850
But if he realized, if
you think a little bit

00:16:56.850 --> 00:17:01.400
its difficult to find 30 years
where so much has changed.

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:02.980
You know the end of the Cold War,

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:04.623
the end of the Soviet empire.

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:10.273
Countries in Eastern Europe
coming into the European Union.

00:17:14.750 --> 00:17:18.150
Opening up of China,
developments in India,

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:20.833
the bricks, the emerging economies.

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:24.890
The fall of a number of dictatorships.

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:26.543
Southern America and beyond.

00:17:29.250 --> 00:17:33.810
You name it, a lot happened
throughout this period

00:17:33.810 --> 00:17:36.633
and in a very fast changing environment.

00:17:37.713 --> 00:17:40.210
I think its very important to understand

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:44.350
how much has changed in
order also to understand

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:48.190
how much resistance
there may be to change.

00:17:48.190 --> 00:17:50.330
And how difficult it is for our societies

00:17:50.330 --> 00:17:55.330
to adapt to such an
accelerated pace of change.

00:17:57.030 --> 00:17:59.470
I think this is something we need to keep

00:17:59.470 --> 00:18:01.920
in the background if we want to understand

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:05.233
some of the challenges that we
have today in our societies.

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:08.840
The second point is exactly that one.

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:11.820
Huge changes during this period.

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:13.833
Take the issue of power for instance.

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:22.690
When we moved from a
basically bipolar world

00:18:22.690 --> 00:18:25.870
during the Cold War to
a uni-polar world during

00:18:25.870 --> 00:18:30.060
a number of years where your
country was predominant.

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:31.830
To a situation today where we have

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:35.040
an increasing multi-polarity.

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:38.703
Or an a polarity or whatever
you want to call it.

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:44.630
But if you take a look at
power more attentively you see

00:18:44.630 --> 00:18:48.240
a transfer and a sharing and an erosion,

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:50.380
a diffusion of power.

00:18:50.380 --> 00:18:54.040
Moving from west to east, north to south.

00:18:54.040 --> 00:18:58.263
But also moving from
government regulators,

00:18:59.890 --> 00:19:04.163
public authorities towards
business, towards other actors

00:19:07.430 --> 00:19:08.463
in the society.

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:12.470
You know, national governments lost power

00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:14.590
in face of globalization for instance.

00:19:14.590 --> 00:19:18.223
A national regulator has less power today

00:19:18.223 --> 00:19:21.020
than it had before because the scale

00:19:21.020 --> 00:19:23.870
of the reality it has to
deal with is not equipped

00:19:23.870 --> 00:19:25.730
to that scale.

00:19:25.730 --> 00:19:29.440
And we have not yet found
an alternative level

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:33.350
of regulation that
compensates for the weakness

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:36.003
of the national level.

00:19:38.120 --> 00:19:41.270
But we also have witnessed
a diffusion of power

00:19:41.270 --> 00:19:46.270
from mediators, political
parties, trade unions,

00:19:50.340 --> 00:19:54.420
different mechanisms of
mediation within a society

00:19:55.480 --> 00:19:57.483
move from them to the individual.

00:19:58.870 --> 00:20:02.820
The empowering, or the
empowerment of individuals

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:04.510
in our society.

00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:08.420
So this evolution of power
is extremely interesting

00:20:08.420 --> 00:20:11.600
and it characterizes to a large extent

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:14.340
this period I'm trying to characterize.

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:18.650
But you also have an enormous
and fast paced change

00:20:18.650 --> 00:20:22.540
in our societies, cultural changes.

00:20:22.540 --> 00:20:24.570
Behavioral changes.

00:20:24.570 --> 00:20:26.500
The way we approach a number of issues

00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:31.020
that for many centuries were
thought to be just there.

00:20:34.070 --> 00:20:36.580
I'm talking about minorities rights.

00:20:36.580 --> 00:20:38.463
I'm talking about LGBT rights.

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:42.423
I'm talking about same sex marriage.

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:47.930
I'm talking about freedom of religion.

00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:51.140
The inclusion of new religions.

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:54.380
All this has introduced in our societies

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:57.600
and sometimes we don't realize that

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:00.390
if we don't step back a little bit

00:21:00.390 --> 00:21:04.033
and realize how much things have changed.

00:21:05.580 --> 00:21:09.890
Again to try to better
understand that adaptation

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:15.453
is maybe sometimes a
slower process than change.

00:21:16.930 --> 00:21:21.016
And this dichotomy or
this (foreign language)

00:21:21.016 --> 00:21:23.167
as the French would say, between change

00:21:23.167 --> 00:21:27.490
and adaptation is maybe
a source of explanations

00:21:27.490 --> 00:21:29.453
for some of the problems we have today.

00:21:31.870 --> 00:21:35.330
The rise of diversity,
it comes with not only

00:21:35.330 --> 00:21:38.030
the cultural changes
but also with the fact

00:21:38.030 --> 00:21:39.990
that we live in a global world.

00:21:39.990 --> 00:21:42.110
That we have access to information

00:21:42.110 --> 00:21:44.653
to a scale that we never had before.

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:50.140
So what I'm saying here today can be heard

00:21:50.140 --> 00:21:51.543
in China as we speak.

00:21:53.140 --> 00:21:57.280
And what you are doing everyday is having

00:21:57.280 --> 00:21:59.780
an impact around the world.

00:21:59.780 --> 00:22:03.630
The financial crisis was the
vivid illustration of that.

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:07.330
We realized very clearly that you know,

00:22:07.330 --> 00:22:12.020
a subprime mortgage in
California could rock

00:22:12.020 --> 00:22:13.703
the entire financial system.

00:22:14.810 --> 00:22:19.080
And a strike in Chinese ports can disrupt

00:22:19.920 --> 00:22:21.503
the entire supply chain.

00:22:23.730 --> 00:22:27.194
You know a problem in a
country that represents 2%

00:22:27.194 --> 00:22:30.907
of the European Union's GDP can create

00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:35.123
financial waves around the world.

00:22:37.460 --> 00:22:42.330
This is the kind of world
we began to live with

00:22:43.300 --> 00:22:47.490
or to live in, in the last 25 to 30 years.

00:22:47.490 --> 00:22:51.630
But also the fact that we are living

00:22:51.630 --> 00:22:53.763
in a global public space.

00:22:55.470 --> 00:22:57.560
I mean take the issue of
inequality for instance

00:22:57.560 --> 00:23:01.060
which I will refer later
on, but I was discussing

00:23:01.060 --> 00:23:03.560
with some of your professors earlier,

00:23:03.560 --> 00:23:06.580
inequality is there in
some parts of the world

00:23:06.580 --> 00:23:10.700
it has widened, in some
parts it has narrowed.

00:23:10.700 --> 00:23:12.900
But the biggest difference is that people

00:23:12.900 --> 00:23:15.380
are more aware of that inequality today

00:23:15.380 --> 00:23:16.623
than they were before.

00:23:20.140 --> 00:23:23.720
And this is a very
important political factor.

00:23:23.720 --> 00:23:28.720
That maybe times and we
have not yet addressed them.

00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:32.150
Or the heart of these problems

00:23:32.150 --> 00:23:35.230
in a way that at the same
times preserves freedom

00:23:35.230 --> 00:23:38.910
of communication and freedom of expression

00:23:38.910 --> 00:23:42.940
but also ensures the balance
of the public debate.

00:23:42.940 --> 00:23:44.897
Maybe we'll come back to that.

00:23:44.897 --> 00:23:46.860
And the third point I
want to mention about

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:48.930
this period which has an enormous impact

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:53.140
in what we live today and
the world we live in today

00:23:53.140 --> 00:23:56.983
is what happened in the last
five, six, seven, eight years.

00:23:58.470 --> 00:24:00.963
Which I would call the recent turbulence.

00:24:02.720 --> 00:24:05.760
It basically started in 2007, eight, nine

00:24:05.760 --> 00:24:09.170
with the financial crisis,
the economic crisis

00:24:09.170 --> 00:24:12.320
that was associated with
it and the social crisis

00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:15.923
that was a consequence in some countries.

00:24:18.030 --> 00:24:20.850
Which of course exacerbated
some of the problems

00:24:20.850 --> 00:24:25.510
that were there, brought
them to the surface.

00:24:25.510 --> 00:24:29.250
And made us realize that we had sort of

00:24:29.250 --> 00:24:32.190
to catch up with the changes.

00:24:32.190 --> 00:24:35.320
The whole debate and I
was previous capacities

00:24:35.320 --> 00:24:37.297
involved in that, the work we tried to do

00:24:37.297 --> 00:24:41.100
in the G20 to address the financial crisis

00:24:41.100 --> 00:24:46.100
was clear evidence of an
economic reality not matched

00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:51.440
by the governance, the
required governance mechanisms

00:24:52.750 --> 00:24:53.843
to address.

00:24:54.940 --> 00:24:57.193
It was followed by Syria.

00:24:58.840 --> 00:25:00.950
Its been five or six
years that we are dealing

00:25:00.950 --> 00:25:04.293
with an extremely serious crisis in Syria.

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:10.473
500000 people or more
dead out of this conflict.

00:25:11.890 --> 00:25:16.273
10 million displaced people
more than five million refugees.

00:25:19.360 --> 00:25:20.903
And its still ongoing.

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:25.973
Aleppo today is a tragedy.

00:25:29.820 --> 00:25:31.243
And then we had Crimea.

00:25:33.540 --> 00:25:37.173
The annexation of Crimea
by the Russian Federation.

00:25:39.170 --> 00:25:42.673
Which we cannot accept,
which we will not accept.

00:25:43.650 --> 00:25:48.650
But which represents the
end of an understanding

00:25:49.500 --> 00:25:52.730
how the world should behave following

00:25:52.730 --> 00:25:53.930
the end of the Cold War.

00:25:56.113 --> 00:25:58.777
And then we have the
eruption of ISIS, Daesh.

00:25:59.660 --> 00:26:04.660
A new form of terrorism
which qualitatively changed

00:26:05.370 --> 00:26:09.623
the fight against terrorism
that we had been engaged before.

00:26:16.160 --> 00:26:18.710
The bricks, the emerging
economies I mentioned

00:26:18.710 --> 00:26:22.750
as being one of the new
realities of this period

00:26:22.750 --> 00:26:27.490
in the last few years
facing a number of problems.

00:26:27.490 --> 00:26:31.380
So the idea that some had
that the West was decline

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:33.500
and the emerging economies
will be the future

00:26:33.500 --> 00:26:36.453
of our planet, its not
exactly materializing.

00:26:37.580 --> 00:26:40.290
At least not in such a smooth way

00:26:40.290 --> 00:26:42.303
as one could have expected.

00:26:45.560 --> 00:26:48.640
We had the volatility in
the commodities market

00:26:50.270 --> 00:26:54.700
in very pronounced ways
and we're still faced

00:26:54.700 --> 00:26:58.660
with enormous problems in
the banking sector around

00:26:58.660 --> 00:27:02.403
the world in some
countries more than others.

00:27:03.430 --> 00:27:06.890
And recently, even more recently in fact

00:27:06.890 --> 00:27:11.890
in the last semester we
had what I would call

00:27:12.290 --> 00:27:17.290
two political earthquakes
which came on top of all this.

00:27:19.610 --> 00:27:24.610
One was the referendum in
United Kingdom determining

00:27:25.570 --> 00:27:28.110
the future withdrawal of the country

00:27:28.110 --> 00:27:30.140
from the European Union.

00:27:30.140 --> 00:27:34.720
And the other was lets
say the unexpected result

00:27:34.720 --> 00:27:39.250
according to polls of the
presidential elections

00:27:39.250 --> 00:27:40.303
in the United States.

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:45.823
I'm not qualifying either of these events

00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:52.850
and their outcome, I'm
just identifying them

00:27:52.850 --> 00:27:56.845
as relevant political events.

00:27:56.845 --> 00:27:58.640
Which I compare to earthquakes

00:28:00.350 --> 00:28:04.743
because like earthquakes the
were not necessarily foreseen.

00:28:07.150 --> 00:28:12.150
And like earthquakes
they can cause damage,

00:28:13.670 --> 00:28:18.670
they can cause change, and
they can have repercussions.

00:28:20.760 --> 00:28:22.653
They can even have aftershocks.

00:28:24.190 --> 00:28:28.350
So they are from a political
science point of view

00:28:28.350 --> 00:28:31.530
and maybe some political
scientists in the room,

00:28:31.530 --> 00:28:32.963
they are extremely relevant.

00:28:34.053 --> 00:28:39.000
But again I'm not qualifying,
expressing any opinion

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:42.000
about the result of referendum

00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:44.380
or the result of the elections.

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:47.323
That should be very clear.

00:28:50.130 --> 00:28:54.110
So this is where we are
in terms of what I believe

00:28:54.110 --> 00:28:59.040
are three main elements
we should consider today

00:28:59.040 --> 00:29:02.250
if we want to understand
where we are today.

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:07.250
Again, the end of a cycle
of huge, fast change

00:29:08.140 --> 00:29:09.213
in our societies.

00:29:11.740 --> 00:29:15.910
This change is having
political, cultural, societal,

00:29:15.910 --> 00:29:19.660
economic, you name it,
dimensions effecting

00:29:21.070 --> 00:29:23.270
all the aspects of our
life and the organization

00:29:23.270 --> 00:29:26.760
of our societies and we
have recent turbulence

00:29:26.760 --> 00:29:30.647
that came on top of all that to make the--

00:29:32.360 --> 00:29:35.110
What are these fault lines
in a again, telegraphic way?

00:29:36.060 --> 00:29:38.513
Its global versus local.

00:29:39.830 --> 00:29:43.260
Its where the people
think globally and want

00:29:43.260 --> 00:29:48.260
to live globally or
rather want to concentrate

00:29:48.280 --> 00:29:50.730
on a more local dimension.

00:29:50.730 --> 00:29:54.300
Its open versus closed whether we want

00:29:54.300 --> 00:29:57.670
to continue to have or to keep this trend

00:29:57.670 --> 00:29:59.583
of openness of our societies.

00:30:00.480 --> 00:30:02.140
In cultural terms, in economic terms,

00:30:02.140 --> 00:30:07.140
in political terms or if
we want to close down.

00:30:09.300 --> 00:30:12.041
If we think that we open up too much

00:30:12.041 --> 00:30:16.040
and that we have to
correct the cursor somehow.

00:30:17.170 --> 00:30:20.090
Its the tension between the center,

00:30:20.090 --> 00:30:23.563
the political center and the two extremes.

00:30:26.608 --> 00:30:28.158
The far right and the far left.

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:32.870
What is happening to the center?

00:30:32.870 --> 00:30:35.393
Can the center hold
like the poem would say?

00:30:37.593 --> 00:30:39.143
What is the center, by the way?

00:30:40.910 --> 00:30:43.850
You know, questions for discussion.

00:30:43.850 --> 00:30:47.393
Its the tension between
pluralism and populism.

00:30:49.350 --> 00:30:54.210
Pluralism is acceptance
of difference, diversity

00:30:54.210 --> 00:30:59.210
as a source of good for our societies.

00:31:02.410 --> 00:31:07.410
Against populism which is
basically easy solutions

00:31:07.810 --> 00:31:09.323
for complex problems.

00:31:11.880 --> 00:31:13.080
And much more than that.

00:31:14.330 --> 00:31:18.680
But basically a narrower approach

00:31:20.510 --> 00:31:23.193
to the way we organize our lives.

00:31:24.050 --> 00:31:29.050
Its liberal democracy versus, you can call

00:31:29.190 --> 00:31:32.163
it what you want, maybe
authoritarian capitalism.

00:31:33.760 --> 00:31:38.130
Or authoritarian regimes, I don't know.

00:31:38.130 --> 00:31:41.610
Where you combine certain elements

00:31:41.610 --> 00:31:43.780
of capitalism and where people try

00:31:43.780 --> 00:31:47.460
to prove capitalism does
not necessarily require

00:31:47.460 --> 00:31:48.683
a liberal democracy.

00:31:50.240 --> 00:31:53.210
This tension is there, this
debate is there as well.

00:31:53.210 --> 00:31:55.270
There's a debate between multilateralism

00:31:56.540 --> 00:31:59.540
which is what we try to
do at the United Nations

00:31:59.540 --> 00:32:02.170
which is based on the
concept of cooperation

00:32:02.170 --> 00:32:06.003
instead of opposition or conflict.

00:32:07.410 --> 00:32:11.010
Tension between
multilateralism and the idea

00:32:11.010 --> 00:32:13.130
of spheres of influence.

00:32:13.130 --> 00:32:15.583
Geopolitics at its worst.

00:32:17.690 --> 00:32:19.100
This fault line is also there.

00:32:19.100 --> 00:32:21.470
So a number of areas in which you know

00:32:21.470 --> 00:32:23.787
the cleavage, the fault lines I think

00:32:23.787 --> 00:32:28.160
is the best term I can
find in my poor english

00:32:28.160 --> 00:32:32.020
to define these tensions in our societies.

00:32:32.020 --> 00:32:35.740
Where the debate is taking place.

00:32:35.740 --> 00:32:38.860
The confrontation is taking place

00:32:38.860 --> 00:32:43.860
and where sometime, somehow
a new consensus must emerge.

00:32:48.650 --> 00:32:49.670
This means what?

00:32:49.670 --> 00:32:51.890
This means that some
of the old assumptions

00:32:51.890 --> 00:32:55.780
that we had about the way
we organize our countries

00:32:55.780 --> 00:32:58.520
in the world are obsolete somehow.

00:32:58.520 --> 00:33:00.630
You know, the right, left divide.

00:33:00.630 --> 00:33:03.930
What is right and left
today in our societies?

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:08.240
Tell me in your country,
in our countries in Europe

00:33:08.240 --> 00:33:10.083
how do you define right and left?

00:33:13.010 --> 00:33:15.550
I take the issue of trade, of free trade.

00:33:15.550 --> 00:33:17.870
The far left and the
far right have exactly

00:33:17.870 --> 00:33:19.113
the same position.

00:33:22.860 --> 00:33:23.810
They don't want it.

00:33:25.650 --> 00:33:27.000
Exactly the same arguments.

00:33:29.830 --> 00:33:33.910
Difficult to distinguish what
is left and what is right.

00:33:33.910 --> 00:33:37.483
The big ideologies of the 20th
century are basically gone.

00:33:39.190 --> 00:33:41.633
That debate is over to a large extent.

00:33:43.620 --> 00:33:47.210
But we are struggling to find concepts

00:33:47.210 --> 00:33:51.350
that sort of organize our thoughts

00:33:51.350 --> 00:33:54.880
and organize ourselves
around those concepts.

00:33:54.880 --> 00:33:57.260
Its very interesting because we are

00:33:57.260 --> 00:34:01.070
at the same times discussing
very crucial, serious issues

00:34:01.070 --> 00:34:03.690
about the way we organize ourselves

00:34:03.690 --> 00:34:06.873
but the conceptual side is not following.

00:34:09.690 --> 00:34:14.113
Its maybe a transition period
but a very interesting one.

00:34:15.400 --> 00:34:20.400
But all this creates space
for I mean two realities

00:34:20.540 --> 00:34:22.360
that we see coming up very strongly.

00:34:22.360 --> 00:34:26.263
One is the role of religions
in a political debate.

00:34:28.010 --> 00:34:29.573
And the role of nationalism.

00:34:31.380 --> 00:34:36.100
And you know, lets see how this evolves

00:34:36.100 --> 00:34:41.100
but its part and its linked
to the evolution we've seen

00:34:43.140 --> 00:34:45.460
in the political debate.

00:34:45.460 --> 00:34:47.350
Another reality today, and I mentioned

00:34:47.350 --> 00:34:50.960
it briefly earlier is the role of emotion

00:34:50.960 --> 00:34:54.433
and perception in the political arena.

00:34:56.930 --> 00:35:01.523
But also manipulation of
emotions and perception.

00:35:03.010 --> 00:35:06.110
This is, you can tell me
it happens all the time

00:35:06.110 --> 00:35:10.190
in politics since Rome
and Greece and beyond.

00:35:10.190 --> 00:35:13.120
Obviously so, this is
part of human nature.

00:35:13.120 --> 00:35:15.299
But I think we are attending degrees

00:35:15.299 --> 00:35:18.590
of relevance of these dimensions

00:35:18.590 --> 00:35:22.070
which we've never seen before.

00:35:22.070 --> 00:35:26.310
And this replaces reason,
this replaces facts

00:35:27.170 --> 00:35:28.963
in our political discourse.

00:35:30.020 --> 00:35:31.480
It has to do with social media.

00:35:31.480 --> 00:35:33.300
It has to do with the different way

00:35:33.300 --> 00:35:35.253
we communicate among ourselves.

00:35:36.210 --> 00:35:40.170
It has to do with the
simple fact, for instance,

00:35:40.170 --> 00:35:43.570
that we tend to listen only
to the people we agree with

00:35:45.090 --> 00:35:47.250
and we tend to read and tweet

00:35:47.250 --> 00:35:51.267
and retweet comfortably
only with the people

00:35:55.470 --> 00:35:56.493
that we agree with.

00:35:59.490 --> 00:36:04.050
This is not good because
we are crystallizing

00:36:04.050 --> 00:36:04.883
the differences.

00:36:04.883 --> 00:36:06.680
We are not reducing the difference.

00:36:06.680 --> 00:36:10.330
We are not approaching the opposite sides

00:36:10.330 --> 00:36:12.350
of an argument, you are crystallizing

00:36:13.840 --> 00:36:16.683
these boxes of understanding of reality.

00:36:18.080 --> 00:36:20.870
When I was, I don't want
to talk too much about

00:36:20.870 --> 00:36:23.410
the United States but I
think this is a good example.

00:36:23.410 --> 00:36:25.160
Again it happens in Europe as well.

00:36:26.140 --> 00:36:29.770
I was following the campaign in 2012.

00:36:29.770 --> 00:36:32.230
I was ambassador to the United States

00:36:32.230 --> 00:36:35.910
and I went to some
meetings and one day I went

00:36:35.910 --> 00:36:39.910
to both sides and it was very interesting

00:36:39.910 --> 00:36:43.070
to see that in one side
there were watching

00:36:43.070 --> 00:36:48.060
only one TV channel on the
other side they watching

00:36:48.060 --> 00:36:50.843
only one TV channel, they
were different TV channels.

00:36:52.629 --> 00:36:57.629
And this for me was an
anecdotal, empirical illustration

00:36:59.880 --> 00:37:04.060
of a larger and much
more important reality

00:37:04.060 --> 00:37:09.060
which is that we
increasingly live in silos

00:37:09.740 --> 00:37:14.570
and its not a very comfortable
place to be in a silo, right?

00:37:14.570 --> 00:37:18.320
And it doesn't add and
doesn't help finding

00:37:18.320 --> 00:37:19.973
the solutions that we need today.

00:37:21.110 --> 00:37:26.110
The issue of inequality is
I think extremely important.

00:37:28.160 --> 00:37:32.763
And I think we have all
overlooked it for too long.

00:37:34.670 --> 00:37:39.670
In the sense that you
know, I think we thought

00:37:41.710 --> 00:37:46.710
that economic development,
growth in trade,

00:37:47.420 --> 00:37:51.840
a liberalization, growth will eventually

00:37:53.300 --> 00:37:54.880
solve all the problems.

00:37:54.880 --> 00:37:59.880
Will eventually spread out
in more or less equal terms

00:38:01.810 --> 00:38:05.350
around our societies,
that is not the case.

00:38:05.350 --> 00:38:07.260
We have seen globally a reduction

00:38:07.260 --> 00:38:09.480
in inequality, if you consider

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:14.350
the overall big balances,
equilibriums around the world.

00:38:14.350 --> 00:38:16.300
Again we lifted hundreds of millions

00:38:16.300 --> 00:38:17.663
of people out of poverty.

00:38:18.800 --> 00:38:21.440
And that should not be underestimated

00:38:21.440 --> 00:38:24.060
as a positive result of
the period I'm trying

00:38:24.060 --> 00:38:29.060
to describe but we have
increased inequality

00:38:29.100 --> 00:38:33.300
in certain countries,
certain parts of the world.

00:38:33.300 --> 00:38:35.743
And we have not yet addressed that issue.

00:38:36.710 --> 00:38:39.690
In a way that increased inequality,

00:38:39.690 --> 00:38:43.580
which by the way is exacerbated
by an increased awareness

00:38:43.580 --> 00:38:48.140
of that inequality
because of the circulation

00:38:48.140 --> 00:38:53.140
of information, is now
seriously threatening

00:38:53.890 --> 00:38:56.720
the very principle of globalization.

00:38:56.720 --> 00:38:59.540
And the very principle of free trade

00:38:59.540 --> 00:39:02.290
and opening of our economies.

00:39:02.290 --> 00:39:07.290
So for that reason as much
as for the very good reasons

00:39:08.730 --> 00:39:10.900
of addressing the issue of people living

00:39:10.900 --> 00:39:13.020
in difficult circumstances, I think this

00:39:13.020 --> 00:39:15.250
is one of the major reasons that

00:39:15.250 --> 00:39:20.250
the international community
needs to address and soon.

00:39:24.150 --> 00:39:26.920
In the mean time there
is a world out there.

00:39:26.920 --> 00:39:28.730
There is a planet out there

00:39:29.800 --> 00:39:34.800
and there I think this is
something that is not changing.

00:39:38.410 --> 00:39:40.360
It has not changed and will not change.

00:39:42.240 --> 00:39:46.740
Its clear for me that the
single most important thing

00:39:46.740 --> 00:39:49.653
that unites us is not a party.

00:39:51.530 --> 00:39:54.080
It is not a tribe, its not a gender,

00:39:54.080 --> 00:39:57.383
its not a conviction,
its not a university.

00:39:59.080 --> 00:40:02.870
Its not a guru, its not a god.

00:40:02.870 --> 00:40:06.270
What really unites us all is the fact

00:40:06.270 --> 00:40:10.090
that there is so far only one place

00:40:10.090 --> 00:40:12.340
in the universe where we can live

00:40:12.340 --> 00:40:14.373
and that is called planet earth.

00:40:15.780 --> 00:40:17.833
There is no planet B.

00:40:19.220 --> 00:40:23.250
So this is a reality that has not changed

00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:24.640
in the last 30 years.

00:40:24.640 --> 00:40:27.520
It will not change unless we found

00:40:27.520 --> 00:40:29.586
a way to live on Mars.

00:40:29.586 --> 00:40:31.586
It will not change in the next 30 years.

00:40:33.420 --> 00:40:37.183
It may change if we don't take care of it.

00:40:39.110 --> 00:40:41.110
It may change in the wrong direction

00:40:41.110 --> 00:40:43.550
that we can no longer
live in the only place

00:40:43.550 --> 00:40:47.100
where we are supposed
to live in this universe

00:40:47.100 --> 00:40:52.100
and this is of course the whole
debate about climate change,

00:40:55.020 --> 00:40:58.613
pollution, biodiversity, you name it.

00:41:00.920 --> 00:41:04.710
And here again in this area, this period

00:41:04.710 --> 00:41:09.710
that just preceded us,
has been extremely rich.

00:41:10.210 --> 00:41:12.030
And we have made enormous progress.

00:41:12.030 --> 00:41:15.470
And I think in this school
of, very much linked

00:41:15.470 --> 00:41:20.470
to environment, we must
recognize, we must rejoice

00:41:23.390 --> 00:41:25.910
about the fact that we
made enormous progress

00:41:27.130 --> 00:41:31.210
in awareness and
effective political action

00:41:32.450 --> 00:41:34.063
to fight climate change.

00:41:37.890 --> 00:41:42.890
We are a long way from what
we had in the 60s and the 70s

00:41:45.380 --> 00:41:49.480
where those of us, maybe those of you who

00:41:49.480 --> 00:41:52.030
were talking about these issues were seen

00:41:52.030 --> 00:41:56.463
as radicals, eccentrics,
and worse things than that.

00:41:59.790 --> 00:42:02.170
This now at the center
of the political debate

00:42:02.170 --> 00:42:04.863
the center of the political decision.

00:42:05.770 --> 00:42:08.623
I'm very proud of the European
Union's role in all this.

00:42:11.920 --> 00:42:13.233
We have been pioneers.

00:42:15.220 --> 00:42:19.290
We started our first
environmental action program

00:42:19.290 --> 00:42:22.373
in European Union, goes back to 1973.

00:42:23.260 --> 00:42:26.690
There was some important
developments around those days also

00:42:26.690 --> 00:42:31.063
in the West but with less
obvious political consequences.

00:42:32.670 --> 00:42:37.580
So it is not that old but we have today

00:42:38.570 --> 00:42:41.743
a global agreement on the
fight against climate change.

00:42:43.100 --> 00:42:43.933
Its quite impressive.

00:42:43.933 --> 00:42:48.450
I remember very well in 2007 I was Chief

00:42:48.450 --> 00:42:50.820
of Staff of President
of European Commission

00:42:50.820 --> 00:42:54.033
which is a sort of European
executive government.

00:42:54.950 --> 00:42:59.720
And we put together the
first meaningful climate

00:42:59.720 --> 00:43:01.753
slash energy package.

00:43:03.700 --> 00:43:06.150
And so we approved it, and now we're going

00:43:06.150 --> 00:43:08.470
to our member states and
then we started talking

00:43:08.470 --> 00:43:10.900
to our partners around the world.

00:43:10.900 --> 00:43:12.800
Because typically there ain't much you

00:43:12.800 --> 00:43:15.653
can do about climate change
if you don't do it together.

00:43:19.310 --> 00:43:24.083
So we started talking and
we were pretty much alone.

00:43:25.670 --> 00:43:27.900
I looked to my right, I looked to my left,

00:43:27.900 --> 00:43:31.893
I looked back and I didn't
see any people following us.

00:43:32.830 --> 00:43:37.830
We went to the G8 and
apart from the Europeans

00:43:38.980 --> 00:43:40.093
not much happening.

00:43:41.360 --> 00:43:44.193
Most of the people were in
denial about climate change.

00:43:46.830 --> 00:43:50.060
And then progressively,
gradually we build up

00:43:50.060 --> 00:43:54.590
a case and that's where we are today.

00:43:54.590 --> 00:43:58.470
Our approach is a mix of
regulatory initiatives,

00:44:01.770 --> 00:44:05.810
legislation, market mechanisms,
extremely important,

00:44:05.810 --> 00:44:09.363
and financial support for
adaptation and otherwise.

00:44:11.560 --> 00:44:13.023
Its been successful.

00:44:15.240 --> 00:44:19.300
Its not been easy, Copenhagen
was not a glorious moment.

00:44:19.300 --> 00:44:22.293
Paris was a great success.

00:44:24.270 --> 00:44:29.270
We have 2020 goals, 2030 goals, 2050 goals

00:44:29.490 --> 00:44:30.603
in the European Union.

00:44:32.880 --> 00:44:35.393
Paris Agreement is great
but its not enough.

00:44:38.950 --> 00:44:42.010
First we need to make sure
that it is really implemented

00:44:43.640 --> 00:44:47.003
and then we have to reassess
and see how much we need to do.

00:44:49.790 --> 00:44:52.250
But first and foremost we need

00:44:52.250 --> 00:44:55.130
to preserve the Paris Agreement.

00:44:55.130 --> 00:44:57.900
We need to make sure that it goes forward

00:44:57.900 --> 00:45:01.900
with a degree of consensus,
international consensus

00:45:01.900 --> 00:45:06.293
between developed and developing
and emerging countries.

00:45:07.350 --> 00:45:12.350
This is a critical challenge
that we need to focus on today.

00:45:15.000 --> 00:45:20.000
I was very happy, excuse me
for this personal remark,

00:45:21.100 --> 00:45:23.530
I think it was in the beginning of October

00:45:23.530 --> 00:45:27.130
when I got green light from Brussels

00:45:27.130 --> 00:45:31.130
to present the instruments of ratification

00:45:31.130 --> 00:45:34.570
by the European Union
of the Treaty of Paris,

00:45:34.570 --> 00:45:35.720
of the Paris Agreement.

00:45:36.660 --> 00:45:41.660
And I went to the treaty
room in the United Nations

00:45:42.110 --> 00:45:45.210
and officially presented the documents.

00:45:45.210 --> 00:45:48.920
And this very simple,
modest fact, you know

00:45:48.920 --> 00:45:53.393
a guy in a dark blue suit
looking bureaucrat, whatever.

00:45:54.330 --> 00:45:59.010
Giving a piece of paper
to some other bureaucrat

00:45:59.010 --> 00:46:03.970
on the other side, this
simple gesture triggered

00:46:05.130 --> 00:46:08.160
the entry into force
of the Paris Agreement

00:46:08.160 --> 00:46:09.610
in the beginning of November.

00:46:10.550 --> 00:46:15.550
So this was very meaningful
for me personally.

00:46:15.900 --> 00:46:18.280
Very meaningful for the European Union

00:46:18.280 --> 00:46:22.810
that those of us who
were sort of pioneering

00:46:23.960 --> 00:46:26.670
the fight against climate
change, at the political

00:46:26.670 --> 00:46:29.370
and institutional level were also

00:46:29.370 --> 00:46:33.990
the ones that triggered
the entry into force

00:46:33.990 --> 00:46:35.613
of the Paris Agreement.

00:46:36.710 --> 00:46:40.490
But its not enough that
it enters into force,

00:46:40.490 --> 00:46:42.400
we need to implement it.

00:46:42.400 --> 00:46:47.370
Today we have I guess, 111 or
12 countries that ratified.

00:46:47.370 --> 00:46:51.980
This corresponds to more
than 75% of the CO2 emissions

00:46:51.980 --> 00:46:52.830
around the world.

00:46:54.200 --> 00:46:58.110
If it stays like that, if it
grows because other countries

00:46:58.110 --> 00:47:01.110
will ratify, because many
other countries have signed,

00:47:01.110 --> 00:47:02.483
we are on the right track.

00:47:03.870 --> 00:47:08.470
And I'm encouraged by what
I hear from everywhere

00:47:09.500 --> 00:47:13.120
that people intend to
pursue on that track.

00:47:13.120 --> 00:47:14.160
This is the right track.

00:47:14.160 --> 00:47:17.540
So as far as we are
concerned, European Union,

00:47:17.540 --> 00:47:22.300
we did our homework, we inspired others,

00:47:22.300 --> 00:47:27.300
we led a coalition, we brought
along the United States

00:47:28.690 --> 00:47:33.200
and China critical
elements of this coalition

00:47:33.200 --> 00:47:35.170
behind the fight against climate change

00:47:36.260 --> 00:47:38.600
and now we have a deal and the deal

00:47:38.600 --> 00:47:41.780
is now being implemented.

00:47:41.780 --> 00:47:44.740
Ministers met in Marrakesh a few days ago

00:47:44.740 --> 00:47:47.190
on the first ever meeting of the parties

00:47:47.190 --> 00:47:48.940
of the Paris Agreement.

00:47:48.940 --> 00:47:51.110
Its on track, it should go on.

00:47:51.110 --> 00:47:55.730
It makes a lot of sense
and its a great victory

00:47:55.730 --> 00:48:00.700
of this period of the
last two or three decades

00:48:00.700 --> 00:48:05.700
that we did to make sure
it comes to good results.

00:48:08.530 --> 00:48:13.530
So benign view or darker view

00:48:13.610 --> 00:48:16.250
of the state of the world today?

00:48:16.250 --> 00:48:18.290
Its up to you to decide.

00:48:18.290 --> 00:48:21.083
I choose to be optimistic.

00:48:22.770 --> 00:48:27.770
I want to keep hope alive about our world.

00:48:30.380 --> 00:48:35.380
I trust the wisdom of
mankind against all odds.

00:48:40.670 --> 00:48:42.720
I trust the commitment and the energy

00:48:42.720 --> 00:48:43.960
of the younger generation

00:48:43.960 --> 00:48:46.683
which is well represented here tonight.

00:48:48.500 --> 00:48:50.733
I am sure they would not let us down.

00:48:51.630 --> 00:48:55.080
And what my generation, the generations

00:48:55.080 --> 00:48:58.430
of my parents, those of
us who lived after the war

00:48:59.850 --> 00:49:03.600
and built the world as it is today,

00:49:03.600 --> 00:49:06.903
I think we did a good
job to be very frank.

00:49:09.190 --> 00:49:11.870
We solved some problems,
we created others.

00:49:11.870 --> 00:49:13.473
That's the nature of things.

00:49:14.470 --> 00:49:19.370
But I trust that we can
with a younger generation

00:49:19.370 --> 00:49:21.013
build an even better world.

00:49:22.900 --> 00:49:27.900
I take this very meaningful
award, the Chubb award

00:49:28.550 --> 00:49:31.130
and the fact that we invited me here

00:49:31.130 --> 00:49:34.770
as an additional incentive to continue

00:49:34.770 --> 00:49:37.530
my modest contribution
at the United Nations

00:49:37.530 --> 00:49:41.650
inside the European Union
to make this a better world.

00:49:41.650 --> 00:49:44.135
I'll trust that will be the case.

00:49:44.135 --> 00:49:47.080
That we'll overcome the
difficulties we have today

00:49:47.080 --> 00:49:49.660
and that if you invite
me in five years time

00:49:49.660 --> 00:49:52.640
as a simple observer
we will have certainly

00:49:52.640 --> 00:49:55.240
a good discussion here about the fact

00:49:55.240 --> 00:49:59.690
that the world is, the benign view

00:49:59.690 --> 00:50:02.010
is maybe the one that we should endorse.

00:50:02.010 --> 00:50:03.900
And that we'll continue
on the right track.

00:50:03.900 --> 00:50:05.430
Thank you very much.

00:50:05.430 --> 00:50:07.680
(applause)

00:50:13.204 --> 00:50:14.871
Thank you, thank you

00:50:16.211 --> 00:50:18.461
(applause)

00:50:20.040 --> 00:50:23.960
- So the ambassador has generously agreed

00:50:23.960 --> 00:50:25.360
to take questions.

00:50:25.360 --> 00:50:27.770
I think when all of you
walked into the auditorium

00:50:27.770 --> 00:50:30.890
you should have received
a card and a pencil.

00:50:30.890 --> 00:50:34.140
So I ask now that you
fill out your questions

00:50:34.140 --> 00:50:37.130
and one of the aids will come and collect

00:50:37.130 --> 00:50:39.120
and I will be stationed over there

00:50:39.120 --> 00:50:40.410
to read your question.

00:50:40.410 --> 00:50:43.203
So I will return the
podium to the ambassador.

00:50:44.350 --> 00:50:45.183
- Thank you.

00:50:55.761 --> 00:50:57.530
- [Mary] So, is this on?

00:50:57.530 --> 00:51:00.900
So while we're waiting I do
have a question to start us off.

00:51:00.900 --> 00:51:04.710
If you are ready, have
you caught your breath?

00:51:04.710 --> 00:51:05.543
- Absolutely.

00:51:07.660 --> 00:51:10.310
- [Mary] So I too would
love to be in the camp

00:51:10.310 --> 00:51:12.670
of the benign view and
the optimistic view.

00:51:12.670 --> 00:51:16.280
So would love to hear
a little bit more about

00:51:16.280 --> 00:51:18.450
the last point you left us off with.

00:51:18.450 --> 00:51:21.660
With the Paris Agreement and what more--

00:51:21.660 --> 00:51:23.900
I would love to hear a
little bit more about

00:51:23.900 --> 00:51:26.037
the question of corporate power

00:51:26.037 --> 00:51:29.280
and the concerns about the
ways in which multinationals

00:51:29.280 --> 00:51:32.940
may or may not comply
with the Paris Agreement

00:51:32.940 --> 00:51:35.090
and reduction of emissions.

00:51:35.090 --> 00:51:37.200
So I'm curious if you think there

00:51:37.200 --> 00:51:40.920
is a particular challenge
that extends beyond

00:51:40.920 --> 00:51:43.000
the diplomatic community
but most certainly

00:51:43.000 --> 00:51:45.240
in terms of the corporate
business communities

00:51:45.240 --> 00:51:48.590
and how to get compliance as you create

00:51:48.590 --> 00:51:51.580
that lovely image of
bureaucrats passing paper,

00:51:51.580 --> 00:51:53.900
I would love to hear
more about the mechanisms

00:51:53.900 --> 00:51:57.260
of compliance and control
that you're envisioning.

00:51:57.260 --> 00:51:59.310
If we are imagining a world where

00:51:59.310 --> 00:52:02.250
the benign view, and
that the Paris Agreement

00:52:02.250 --> 00:52:04.100
will indeed go forward, and it will

00:52:04.100 --> 00:52:06.610
be ratified fully, thank you.

00:52:06.610 --> 00:52:08.950
- Yeah I think the issue is,

00:52:08.950 --> 00:52:11.697
and its a little bit the
history of the European Union

00:52:11.697 --> 00:52:14.230
and our success is a combination

00:52:14.230 --> 00:52:19.230
of sort of top down
regulatory, legislative action.

00:52:19.270 --> 00:52:21.790
Which is absolutely indispensable,

00:52:21.790 --> 00:52:26.790
with market mechanisms
and market common sense.

00:52:29.480 --> 00:52:33.170
I think what we managed to
do inside the European Union,

00:52:33.170 --> 00:52:37.190
for instance bringing
together energy and climate.

00:52:37.190 --> 00:52:41.220
Was to create the right,
lets say narrative.

00:52:41.220 --> 00:52:44.450
The right case, the right concept

00:52:44.450 --> 00:52:46.370
for the economic operators

00:52:46.370 --> 00:52:49.240
which is the ones you're referring to.

00:52:49.240 --> 00:52:53.493
To understand where their
interests would lie.

00:52:54.900 --> 00:52:57.900
And I think what we're doing
with climate change today

00:52:58.750 --> 00:53:01.150
if you take the case
of China for instance,

00:53:01.150 --> 00:53:06.150
is the understanding
that there are benefits

00:53:06.670 --> 00:53:11.670
to be gained by embarking
in this new process.

00:53:12.950 --> 00:53:15.050
So its not only compliance in the sense

00:53:15.050 --> 00:53:19.530
of top down you know regulatory pressure

00:53:19.530 --> 00:53:22.090
on economic operators, its the combination

00:53:22.090 --> 00:53:24.560
of that with the actually interest

00:53:24.560 --> 00:53:25.860
of the economic operators.

00:53:26.800 --> 00:53:29.300
That's why I'm so confident
that the Paris Agreement

00:53:29.300 --> 00:53:34.300
will evolve because there is
a strong economic case for it.

00:53:34.680 --> 00:53:37.450
And you have to couple the Paris Agreement

00:53:37.450 --> 00:53:39.610
with another very interesting product

00:53:39.610 --> 00:53:43.060
of multilateralism which
is the agenda 2030,

00:53:43.060 --> 00:53:44.860
the sustainable development goals.

00:53:44.860 --> 00:53:48.160
The 17, and I invite you to read them.

00:53:48.160 --> 00:53:51.110
Its, I'm always impressed when I go back

00:53:51.110 --> 00:53:54.300
to these 17 sustainable development goals.

00:53:54.300 --> 00:53:58.290
Its a wonderful agenda and
if we implement all that

00:53:58.290 --> 00:53:59.460
the world is certainly going

00:53:59.460 --> 00:54:01.510
to be a better place in a few years time.

00:54:01.510 --> 00:54:04.660
But if you combine the two you have

00:54:04.660 --> 00:54:08.620
a very strong economic
case for the development

00:54:08.620 --> 00:54:12.580
of a green economy, to the development

00:54:12.580 --> 00:54:16.520
of a sustainable way
of using our resources

00:54:16.520 --> 00:54:20.080
and you know, come on.

00:54:20.080 --> 00:54:21.820
Its very difficult to live in Beijing

00:54:21.820 --> 00:54:23.313
and New Delhi these days.

00:54:25.720 --> 00:54:29.410
With the degree of
environmental pollution.

00:54:29.410 --> 00:54:33.260
And its not me coming
from a developed world

00:54:33.260 --> 00:54:37.470
in a nice blue suit saying
this to people suffering there

00:54:37.470 --> 00:54:40.680
its them saying that they
don't want to live like that.

00:54:40.680 --> 00:54:43.800
And the political pressure will be such

00:54:43.800 --> 00:54:47.370
and is already such in
these and other countries

00:54:48.510 --> 00:54:51.130
to the political authorities
to move in the right direction.

00:54:51.130 --> 00:54:54.060
I think the evolution of
China, which I praise here

00:54:55.550 --> 00:54:58.020
as far as climate is concerned.

00:54:58.020 --> 00:55:00.463
Is a good illustration of that.

00:55:01.670 --> 00:55:03.780
You know, they moved from a situation

00:55:03.780 --> 00:55:07.450
in which back in the early days

00:55:07.450 --> 00:55:11.830
of the climate negotiations
where they basically

00:55:11.830 --> 00:55:16.460
were saying, you made it
you have to pay, full stop.

00:55:16.460 --> 00:55:18.570
To a position in which they understand we

00:55:18.570 --> 00:55:21.855
did not necessarily make it for a number

00:55:21.855 --> 00:55:26.855
of decades, even centuries,
but we are certainly making

00:55:27.920 --> 00:55:30.620
it now and we want to benefit from

00:55:30.620 --> 00:55:34.880
this new phase, you know
leap frog, move forward

00:55:34.880 --> 00:55:37.160
in terms of the use of new technologies.

00:55:37.160 --> 00:55:42.020
Create a new set, a new environment

00:55:42.020 --> 00:55:44.690
that will help our environment and I think

00:55:44.690 --> 00:55:47.330
this change in the Chinese position

00:55:47.330 --> 00:55:50.410
which again I praise,
is a good illustration

00:55:50.410 --> 00:55:55.200
of how reality comes to haunt you

00:55:55.200 --> 00:55:58.940
and reality imposes on you a
number of political choices.

00:55:58.940 --> 00:56:02.420
So I think combination
of regulatory pressure

00:56:02.420 --> 00:56:06.520
and the economic evidence of benefit

00:56:06.520 --> 00:56:09.900
on the medium and longterm will bring us

00:56:09.900 --> 00:56:11.813
to the right situation.

00:56:14.270 --> 00:56:16.600
- [Mary] Okay I now
have a lot of questions

00:56:16.600 --> 00:56:19.800
so apologies if we do
not get to your question.

00:56:19.800 --> 00:56:21.650
I'll do my best to get through these.

00:56:23.870 --> 00:56:27.697
So testing your skills as
a diplomat at this point,

00:56:27.697 --> 00:56:31.600
what kind of influences
from the American campaign

00:56:31.600 --> 00:56:36.578
result to Asian countries,
especially China?

00:56:36.578 --> 00:56:38.440
I think the question may be affect

00:56:38.440 --> 00:56:40.450
Asian countries especially China.

00:56:40.450 --> 00:56:42.840
How do you think China
and other Asian countries

00:56:42.840 --> 00:56:45.640
will be getting involved in
sustainable development goals?

00:56:45.640 --> 00:56:47.220
You started to explain
a little bit of that.

00:56:47.220 --> 00:56:49.730
- No I think, what I see
with my Chinese colleagues

00:56:49.730 --> 00:56:53.660
in New York at the United Nations

00:56:53.660 --> 00:56:55.710
is a very strong engagement of China

00:56:55.710 --> 00:56:57.393
in the multilateral system.

00:56:58.690 --> 00:57:01.050
I think there has been a change.

00:57:01.050 --> 00:57:03.810
The way the Chinese diplomats engage

00:57:03.810 --> 00:57:05.690
in all the discussions.

00:57:05.690 --> 00:57:09.090
Their commitment to our core agenda.

00:57:09.090 --> 00:57:10.730
We have our differences of course.

00:57:10.730 --> 00:57:14.542
Lets be clear about that.

00:57:14.542 --> 00:57:18.180
But as far as multilateralism is concerned

00:57:18.180 --> 00:57:21.230
as far as the role of UN is concerned.

00:57:21.230 --> 00:57:23.090
You know that China is becoming very fast

00:57:23.090 --> 00:57:25.010
the most important financial contributor

00:57:25.010 --> 00:57:26.233
to the United Nations.

00:57:27.720 --> 00:57:30.760
They are very active on
peace keeping operations

00:57:30.760 --> 00:57:32.540
and in many other areas.

00:57:32.540 --> 00:57:36.340
And again I saw the report of the meeting

00:57:36.340 --> 00:57:39.000
of the next Secretary
General of the United Nations

00:57:39.000 --> 00:57:41.633
which by the way is a Portuguese citizen.

00:57:42.840 --> 00:57:43.673
Thank you.

00:57:45.170 --> 00:57:48.130
And an excellent person and he'll

00:57:48.130 --> 00:57:49.830
be an excellent Secretary General.

00:57:51.460 --> 00:57:54.890
Reports of this meeting in China yesterday

00:57:54.890 --> 00:57:55.963
and the day before.

00:57:57.340 --> 00:57:59.960
You know, look at that because it shows

00:57:59.960 --> 00:58:03.613
a great deal of convergence between China

00:58:03.613 --> 00:58:05.860
and the United Nations agenda.

00:58:05.860 --> 00:58:07.600
Again, we have our differences in a number

00:58:07.600 --> 00:58:10.140
of areas but I'm confident that China

00:58:10.140 --> 00:58:12.470
will play an increasingly important role

00:58:12.470 --> 00:58:14.850
in the multilateral system including

00:58:14.850 --> 00:58:17.040
on the climate change agenda

00:58:17.040 --> 00:58:18.940
and the sustainable development goals.

00:58:20.760 --> 00:58:22.680
- [Mary] So there are
several questions here

00:58:22.680 --> 00:58:25.880
that are about the EU specifically

00:58:25.880 --> 00:58:29.370
and particularly around,
I assume these are coming

00:58:29.370 --> 00:58:31.220
out of questions around Brexit.

00:58:31.220 --> 00:58:32.473
But I'll read a couple of them.

00:58:32.473 --> 00:58:33.613
- What a surprise.

00:58:35.110 --> 00:58:37.420
- [Mary] In a climate of euro skepticism

00:58:37.420 --> 00:58:40.830
what do you see as the future
of the European project?

00:58:40.830 --> 00:58:42.390
And what would you say to the claim

00:58:42.390 --> 00:58:45.830
that the international order
is increasingly technocratic

00:58:45.830 --> 00:58:47.163
and undemocratic?

00:58:48.410 --> 00:58:52.280
- Well first of all,
well I've been working

00:58:52.280 --> 00:58:54.940
for the European Union I think
now for 30 something years

00:58:54.940 --> 00:58:58.621
so you don't expect me to
say this is not a good idea.

00:58:58.621 --> 00:59:00.420
(laughing)

00:59:00.420 --> 00:59:02.533
I actually think its a great idea.

00:59:05.080 --> 00:59:07.630
I think the construction
of the European Union

00:59:07.630 --> 00:59:11.750
is the most significant
conflict prevention,

00:59:11.750 --> 00:59:14.120
peace building, and
peace keeping operation

00:59:14.120 --> 00:59:16.200
in the history of mankind.

00:59:16.200 --> 00:59:17.473
You can quote me on this.

00:59:18.390 --> 00:59:20.397
Its a nice quote by the way.

00:59:20.397 --> 00:59:22.647
(applause)

00:59:23.687 --> 00:59:25.960
But in fact if you see that we were able

00:59:25.960 --> 00:59:29.820
to bring, we I mean, all the generations

00:59:29.820 --> 00:59:31.410
that contributed to it.

00:59:31.410 --> 00:59:34.460
Think of where Europe was
after the second world war.

00:59:34.460 --> 00:59:36.580
Think of where Europe is today.

00:59:36.580 --> 00:59:39.293
We started with six
countries, we now have 28.

00:59:40.190 --> 00:59:41.510
We brought all the countries

00:59:41.510 --> 00:59:46.160
that were behind the iron
curtain into a democratic space.

00:59:46.160 --> 00:59:48.130
Countries like Portugal
and Spain or Greece

00:59:48.130 --> 00:59:51.810
that lived with right wing dictatorships.

00:59:51.810 --> 00:59:55.060
I lived 17 years in a dictatorship.

00:59:55.060 --> 00:59:57.703
So don't tell me that is not an issue.

00:59:59.200 --> 01:00:00.143
It is an issue.

01:00:01.560 --> 01:00:04.615
And this is what Europeans did.

01:00:04.615 --> 01:00:05.700
I mean the European Union did.

01:00:05.700 --> 01:00:09.193
I mean we raised the levels
of prosperity, security,

01:00:10.590 --> 01:00:14.840
social protection,
education in our societies.

01:00:18.210 --> 01:00:21.720
We have today more than
half a billion people living

01:00:21.720 --> 01:00:26.060
in a framework of
democracy, no death penalty,

01:00:26.060 --> 01:00:31.060
individual freedom, rule
of law, market economy,

01:00:31.900 --> 01:00:34.740
strong social protection,
education system,

01:00:34.740 --> 01:00:37.640
environmental awareness
and concern and action.

01:00:37.640 --> 01:00:39.740
This is what European Union is today.

01:00:39.740 --> 01:00:42.330
And it is still appealing
to a number of countries.

01:00:42.330 --> 01:00:43.483
Take the Balkans.

01:00:44.920 --> 01:00:47.830
The prospect of European accession

01:00:47.830 --> 01:00:49.370
or accession to the European Union

01:00:49.370 --> 01:00:52.060
is the single most important driver

01:00:52.060 --> 01:00:55.780
of reconciliation and
change in the Balkans.

01:00:55.780 --> 01:00:56.853
Take Ukraine.

01:00:57.710 --> 01:01:02.110
You saw huge demonstrations with this flag

01:01:03.220 --> 01:01:06.090
more than you see in the
European capitals by the way.

01:01:06.090 --> 01:01:09.980
So a good example from
our Ukrainian friends.

01:01:09.980 --> 01:01:12.900
You see that the idea is still alive.

01:01:12.900 --> 01:01:13.850
Do we have problems?

01:01:13.850 --> 01:01:16.163
Of course we have problems, don't you?

01:01:18.100 --> 01:01:19.250
We all have problems.

01:01:19.250 --> 01:01:21.650
I mean our countries
are difficult to manage.

01:01:21.650 --> 01:01:24.280
The world is in a difficult place today

01:01:24.280 --> 01:01:26.640
as we discussed earlier.

01:01:26.640 --> 01:01:27.790
Of course we have problems.

01:01:27.790 --> 01:01:30.420
But I don't think we
have a better solution

01:01:30.420 --> 01:01:31.653
than staying together.

01:01:32.910 --> 01:01:37.910
I mean someone said one day
that all European countries

01:01:40.300 --> 01:01:43.320
are equal the difference being that some,

01:01:43.320 --> 01:01:46.330
sorry all the European
countries are small.

01:01:46.330 --> 01:01:49.253
The difference is that some
have not yet realized that.

01:01:50.860 --> 01:01:54.300
And the fact is that if you
look at the global scale,

01:01:54.300 --> 01:01:57.250
I mean all individual, taken individual,

01:01:57.250 --> 01:01:59.470
all the European Union
countries are too small

01:01:59.470 --> 01:02:01.423
to face the challenges we have today.

01:02:02.690 --> 01:02:04.750
But together we can count.

01:02:04.750 --> 01:02:06.780
We are the most important economic block

01:02:06.780 --> 01:02:08.053
in world still today.

01:02:09.800 --> 01:02:12.560
More than half a billion people.

01:02:12.560 --> 01:02:17.550
And so I'm confident that we will overcome

01:02:17.550 --> 01:02:20.450
whatever problems, and we have had a few

01:02:21.540 --> 01:02:24.420
in the past few years
as we had in the past.

01:02:24.420 --> 01:02:26.120
But I want to stress one point

01:02:26.120 --> 01:02:28.630
that sometimes is a little bit overlooked.

01:02:28.630 --> 01:02:31.533
Our process is an incremental one.

01:02:33.350 --> 01:02:37.963
That is we move forward as we can.

01:02:39.150 --> 01:02:43.420
We move forward from maybe
one crisis to the other.

01:02:43.420 --> 01:02:46.370
Maybe from one opportunity to the other.

01:02:46.370 --> 01:02:49.570
We don't have a blue print that says, back

01:02:49.570 --> 01:02:54.570
in 1957 we gonna do this,
this and that with this, no.

01:02:55.143 --> 01:03:00.143
We moved forward as the conditions
were favorable for that.

01:03:00.750 --> 01:03:04.520
We slowed down here, we accelerated there

01:03:04.520 --> 01:03:08.410
and we overcame one crisis
here and one crisis there.

01:03:08.410 --> 01:03:11.290
And so I'm very confident
that European Union

01:03:11.290 --> 01:03:14.670
will stay for very long but we need

01:03:14.670 --> 01:03:18.000
to address some important
issues that are there.

01:03:18.000 --> 01:03:21.210
Some are common to United States.

01:03:21.210 --> 01:03:25.020
The rise of populism for
instance, we discussed it earlier.

01:03:25.020 --> 01:03:27.860
Some structural economic issues, you know.

01:03:27.860 --> 01:03:29.303
An aging society.

01:03:30.500 --> 01:03:35.043
The issue of competitiveness,
demography, you know.

01:03:35.880 --> 01:03:39.450
You know them, some of
them you have here as well.

01:03:39.450 --> 01:03:42.160
But I think the sense of belonging

01:03:42.160 --> 01:03:44.260
to a common project.

01:03:44.260 --> 01:03:48.090
The sense of not being able really

01:03:48.090 --> 01:03:53.090
to exercise our full
sovereignty in total isolation

01:03:53.670 --> 01:03:55.290
is a very strong one.

01:03:55.290 --> 01:03:59.070
On Brexit and the consequences
of Brexit some people

01:03:59.070 --> 01:04:03.505
were expecting this to be, to trigger

01:04:03.505 --> 01:04:08.505
a downgrading of the acceptability

01:04:08.990 --> 01:04:11.870
or the enthusiasm of the
support for the European Union.

01:04:11.870 --> 01:04:14.730
The polls show the opposite.

01:04:14.730 --> 01:04:17.000
There has been an increasing support

01:04:17.000 --> 01:04:20.253
for the European Union
since the referendum.

01:04:22.510 --> 01:04:25.250
I'm addressing the issue of
the United States election's

01:04:25.250 --> 01:04:28.697
consequences on the world
because I leave that to you.

01:04:31.640 --> 01:04:34.750
- [Mary] So some of these
questions regard your work

01:04:34.750 --> 01:04:38.340
and your role, in
particular as EU ambassador

01:04:38.340 --> 01:04:42.120
to the UN how hard is it
to reach a common position

01:04:42.120 --> 01:04:44.770
among the 28 members of EU?

01:04:44.770 --> 01:04:47.440
Do you see your role as a facilitator

01:04:47.440 --> 01:04:50.530
of different positions or
more as working towards

01:04:50.530 --> 01:04:52.763
a specific EU vision?

01:04:53.900 --> 01:04:55.550
- Well very interesting question.

01:04:56.570 --> 01:05:01.570
Its you know, I'll try
to describe my role.

01:05:01.930 --> 01:05:05.070
I first have to maximize the impact

01:05:05.070 --> 01:05:07.710
of the European Union by maximizing

01:05:07.710 --> 01:05:11.720
the capacity that we have of
agreeing on common positions.

01:05:11.720 --> 01:05:16.340
That is my first role when I wake up

01:05:16.340 --> 01:05:19.700
is to make sure that
all my 28 sort of more

01:05:19.700 --> 01:05:21.350
or less going the same direction.

01:05:22.260 --> 01:05:26.457
And that's a very stimulating
thought every morning

01:05:29.110 --> 01:05:30.860
in front of the mirror.

01:05:30.860 --> 01:05:34.030
And we actually do achieve quite a lot.

01:05:34.030 --> 01:05:36.930
You know the most recent statistics show

01:05:36.930 --> 01:05:39.420
that more or less that we vote

01:05:39.420 --> 01:05:41.670
the same way, that we
have a common position

01:05:41.670 --> 01:05:46.670
on more than 93 or 94% of
the votes that take place

01:05:47.900 --> 01:05:50.140
or the positions we are to take.

01:05:50.140 --> 01:05:53.120
This is incredibly successful.

01:05:53.120 --> 01:05:55.183
We're talking about 28 countries.

01:05:56.040 --> 01:05:59.560
Spread out in a continental scale

01:05:59.560 --> 01:06:01.880
with different cultures,
different languages,

01:06:01.880 --> 01:06:04.610
different traditions,
different economic interests,

01:06:04.610 --> 01:06:09.447
different dimension coming
together around same position.

01:06:12.230 --> 01:06:15.880
So this is my first and
my team of course, role.

01:06:15.880 --> 01:06:18.380
And for that we do, I chair and host

01:06:18.380 --> 01:06:20.920
a weekly meeting of all the ambassadors

01:06:20.920 --> 01:06:23.300
and my colleagues they keep on
meeting all their colleagues.

01:06:23.300 --> 01:06:27.753
We do more than 1000 meetings
a year or coordination.

01:06:29.450 --> 01:06:30.530
This is the process.

01:06:30.530 --> 01:06:33.960
And then the second stage is
when we have a composition

01:06:33.960 --> 01:06:35.860
I go to the security
council and then speak

01:06:35.860 --> 01:06:37.570
on behalf of the 28.

01:06:37.570 --> 01:06:39.580
Or to the general assembly.

01:06:39.580 --> 01:06:44.580
And my colleagues negotiate
with others on the basis of 28.

01:06:45.060 --> 01:06:46.693
So we represent 28.

01:06:47.720 --> 01:06:52.093
And this is very interesting,
very stimulating.

01:06:54.440 --> 01:06:55.670
Its an honor to do that.

01:06:55.670 --> 01:06:57.510
When I speak on the security council

01:06:57.510 --> 01:07:01.040
on behalf of 28, not bad.

01:07:01.040 --> 01:07:02.993
I think my father would be proud.

01:07:04.611 --> 01:07:07.030
And sometimes, most of time we have

01:07:07.030 --> 01:07:09.180
a number of other countries
that align themselves

01:07:09.180 --> 01:07:10.013
with our position.

01:07:10.013 --> 01:07:11.460
So sometimes I'm speaking on behalf

01:07:11.460 --> 01:07:14.610
of 35 and more countries.

01:07:14.610 --> 01:07:17.610
So this rallying power of European Union

01:07:17.610 --> 01:07:22.610
because we provide the
example, is very strong.

01:07:23.610 --> 01:07:27.090
And so I'm optimistic about the future.

01:07:27.090 --> 01:07:31.610
I'm very happy to do the work I do.

01:07:31.610 --> 01:07:35.160
Its not easy sometimes
but its very rewarding

01:07:35.160 --> 01:07:36.660
when we get there.

01:07:36.660 --> 01:07:38.663
And most of the time we do get there.

01:07:41.110 --> 01:07:43.460
- [Mary] Speaking of security.

01:07:43.460 --> 01:07:46.130
One of the questions is could
you talk about security risks

01:07:46.130 --> 01:07:48.667
the EU currently faces and the,

01:07:53.363 --> 01:07:56.350
I think this and the EU
army in the new future.

01:07:56.350 --> 01:07:58.471
Possible creation of the EU army.

01:07:58.471 --> 01:07:59.304
- Yeah.

01:07:59.304 --> 01:08:01.281
We are not planning to have an EU army.

01:08:01.281 --> 01:08:04.780
That's not in the plans.

01:08:04.780 --> 01:08:07.640
What we are doing and today as we speak

01:08:07.640 --> 01:08:10.330
or a few hours ago in
Brussels, we came out

01:08:10.330 --> 01:08:14.540
with a number of proposals,
what we call European

01:08:14.540 --> 01:08:18.050
Defense Action Plan,
which is basically about

01:08:18.050 --> 01:08:20.220
not creating a European army.

01:08:20.220 --> 01:08:22.010
Again this is not in the books.

01:08:22.010 --> 01:08:24.610
This is not our goal.

01:08:24.610 --> 01:08:28.170
The goal is to strengthen the cooperation

01:08:28.170 --> 01:08:33.170
and the links between
European Union armies.

01:08:33.210 --> 01:08:35.760
The armies of the member states.

01:08:35.760 --> 01:08:40.010
Creating an economic space
for defense procurement,

01:08:40.010 --> 01:08:41.160
for instance.

01:08:41.160 --> 01:08:46.140
So that we concentrate
our meager budgetary means

01:08:46.140 --> 01:08:48.200
in a more efficient way.

01:08:48.200 --> 01:08:52.810
So better output with more
or less the same input.

01:08:52.810 --> 01:08:55.070
This is what we're
talking about, efficiency.

01:08:55.070 --> 01:08:59.090
Effectiveness of our defense effort.

01:08:59.090 --> 01:09:01.830
Because, first part of your question,

01:09:01.830 --> 01:09:05.963
we are facing new security threats.

01:09:08.920 --> 01:09:11.080
And this is part I didn't
have time to develop that

01:09:11.080 --> 01:09:13.950
but this is part of
what has been happening

01:09:13.950 --> 01:09:15.163
in the last few years.

01:09:16.190 --> 01:09:20.313
You know, during the Cold
War we knew our enemy.

01:09:22.240 --> 01:09:23.630
We knew what they looked like.

01:09:23.630 --> 01:09:24.943
We knew their names.

01:09:26.150 --> 01:09:27.820
We had a few spies we could know

01:09:27.820 --> 01:09:29.990
a little bit more than that as well.

01:09:29.990 --> 01:09:32.613
So this was a very familiar
environment, right.

01:09:33.837 --> 01:09:35.290
It was very comfortable in a way.

01:09:35.290 --> 01:09:38.310
You know there was always
this nuclear threat above

01:09:38.310 --> 01:09:43.310
our heads but it was quite
predictable in a certain way.

01:09:44.420 --> 01:09:46.660
What is the situation today?

01:09:46.660 --> 01:09:51.123
I mean most of the time we
don't know who we are fighting.

01:09:52.700 --> 01:09:55.480
We don't know their faces,
we don't know their names.

01:09:55.480 --> 01:09:57.760
They don't even have a territory.

01:09:57.760 --> 01:09:59.610
They don't even have an army.

01:09:59.610 --> 01:10:02.763
They don't even have an
ambassador we can talk to.

01:10:03.750 --> 01:10:08.450
I mean its a very
different sort of situation

01:10:08.450 --> 01:10:12.450
that we have today and if
you look at Europe today

01:10:12.450 --> 01:10:15.153
we have a sort of ring of fire around us.

01:10:17.070 --> 01:10:21.233
You start in Ukraine,
Crimea, you go through Syria.

01:10:23.630 --> 01:10:28.630
Iraq, Libya, northern Africa.

01:10:30.740 --> 01:10:32.733
I mean its not an easy place.

01:10:34.040 --> 01:10:37.663
And its our backyard, its our backyard.

01:10:39.690 --> 01:10:42.473
You know I, no I won't say this one.

01:10:44.420 --> 01:10:48.743
But I see Morocco from my
kitchen window in the garden.

01:10:51.950 --> 01:10:54.007
Not referring to anybody else.

01:10:55.264 --> 01:10:57.702
Just an idea that came to me.

01:10:57.702 --> 01:11:00.735
But that's the reality, we are very close

01:11:00.735 --> 01:11:04.323
to our neighborhood.

01:11:05.340 --> 01:11:07.010
You know you see the migration problem.

01:11:07.010 --> 01:11:08.980
People cross a few miles
in the Mediterranean

01:11:08.980 --> 01:11:13.980
and then they are in Italy, Malta, Spain.

01:11:16.170 --> 01:11:17.770
So this is a reality.

01:11:17.770 --> 01:11:20.040
There the terrorism threat upon Europe

01:11:20.040 --> 01:11:22.530
is even bigger than upon the United States

01:11:22.530 --> 01:11:24.800
as we unfortunately know.

01:11:24.800 --> 01:11:26.550
So we need to address all this.

01:11:26.550 --> 01:11:29.310
We want to address it
in full complimentarity

01:11:29.310 --> 01:11:32.530
with NATO, let there be
not doubts about that.

01:11:32.530 --> 01:11:35.450
This is not an alternative to NATO.

01:11:35.450 --> 01:11:38.374
This is a complimentarity to NATO.

01:11:38.374 --> 01:11:43.200
And so I think there
are new security risks,

01:11:43.200 --> 01:11:46.820
there is new security
awareness and new political

01:11:46.820 --> 01:11:48.400
initiatives taking place in Europe.

01:11:48.400 --> 01:11:50.643
But not towards an EU army.

01:11:53.000 --> 01:11:55.550
- [Mary] These two questions
follow up one aspect

01:11:55.550 --> 01:11:58.140
of what you described, the challenges

01:11:58.140 --> 01:12:01.943
of refugees for example and security.

01:12:03.050 --> 01:12:04.370
I'll read both of them.

01:12:04.370 --> 01:12:06.230
The EU prides itself on the concept

01:12:06.230 --> 01:12:08.750
of open borders that
helps form the foundation

01:12:08.750 --> 01:12:10.040
of the single market.

01:12:10.040 --> 01:12:13.210
Is the movement of refugees
jeopardizing this principle?

01:12:13.210 --> 01:12:16.040
Is adding Turkey the answer

01:12:16.040 --> 01:12:18.570
to the EU's refugee movement problems?

01:12:18.570 --> 01:12:20.440
And then the other question that's similar

01:12:20.440 --> 01:12:23.530
is the free movement of
persons a nonnegotiable

01:12:23.530 --> 01:12:26.423
prerequisite of access
to the single market?

01:12:27.310 --> 01:12:30.233
- Yeah many questions in your question.

01:12:31.470 --> 01:12:33.940
Lets take the refugees situation.

01:12:33.940 --> 01:12:37.651
So I don't want, we
should not underestimate

01:12:37.651 --> 01:12:41.760
the importance and the complexity

01:12:41.760 --> 01:12:46.010
of what we have been facing in
the last two or three years.

01:12:46.010 --> 01:12:47.313
And still face today.

01:12:49.250 --> 01:12:51.540
There's first a humanitarian dimension.

01:12:51.540 --> 01:12:54.390
So people are dying in the Mediterranean.

01:12:54.390 --> 01:12:58.010
People are dying in
crossing towards Europe

01:12:58.010 --> 01:13:02.400
and our first concern
has been to save lives.

01:13:02.400 --> 01:13:04.440
In the Mediterranean for instance.

01:13:04.440 --> 01:13:07.710
To create the best possible
conditions for that.

01:13:07.710 --> 01:13:11.200
To fight smuggling because
people are making money out

01:13:11.200 --> 01:13:15.343
of the sufferance of
refugees and migrants.

01:13:16.540 --> 01:13:18.770
And this is something
that we need to address

01:13:18.770 --> 01:13:22.053
as a matter of priority and
that is what we have been doing.

01:13:23.870 --> 01:13:26.470
The other issue is of
course how do we accommodate

01:13:26.470 --> 01:13:31.470
this influx of people coming to Europe?

01:13:31.740 --> 01:13:35.080
And this is a more complex problem

01:13:35.080 --> 01:13:38.790
because of the nature of our system.

01:13:38.790 --> 01:13:43.760
We have 28 countries
with different degrees

01:13:43.760 --> 01:13:48.760
of awareness, experience,
of diverse societies.

01:13:52.120 --> 01:13:55.940
And some of our countries
simply don't have

01:13:55.940 --> 01:14:00.430
that awareness or that
experience or that capability

01:14:00.430 --> 01:14:04.990
in political terms to
move as fast as others

01:14:04.990 --> 01:14:06.370
in addressing these issues.

01:14:06.370 --> 01:14:10.310
So there's a need for us
to find the right balance

01:14:10.310 --> 01:14:12.940
between different positions.

01:14:12.940 --> 01:14:15.340
The other issue is that
you would not solve

01:14:15.340 --> 01:14:18.670
the issue of refugees and migrants simply

01:14:18.670 --> 01:14:21.920
at the destination end
of the problem, right.

01:14:21.920 --> 01:14:24.810
It has at least two other dimensions.

01:14:24.810 --> 01:14:28.043
The transit countries
and the origin countries.

01:14:29.770 --> 01:14:31.560
So we need to look at the root causes

01:14:31.560 --> 01:14:33.440
of this migration flow.

01:14:33.440 --> 01:14:35.373
One of them is the crisis in Syria.

01:14:36.750 --> 01:14:39.020
That's why we have been so active

01:14:39.020 --> 01:14:41.990
in trying to support political solution

01:14:41.990 --> 01:14:43.140
for the Syrian problem.

01:14:44.912 --> 01:14:47.150
We have supported the UN in their efforts.

01:14:47.150 --> 01:14:50.653
We have engaged ourselves
very actively into this.

01:14:52.370 --> 01:14:55.620
But other root causes
is the underdevelopment

01:14:55.620 --> 01:14:58.450
in for instance, sub-Saharan Africa which

01:14:58.450 --> 01:15:01.920
is the source of many of these migrants.

01:15:01.920 --> 01:15:04.620
So what we are developing right now

01:15:04.620 --> 01:15:09.620
is new creative arrangements with transit

01:15:10.120 --> 01:15:13.080
and origin countries in order to address

01:15:13.080 --> 01:15:14.723
the problem in its entirety.

01:15:16.610 --> 01:15:19.320
And we made an agreement with Turkey

01:15:19.320 --> 01:15:21.360
which is working well.

01:15:21.360 --> 01:15:23.970
And we are now in a new
generation of agreements

01:15:23.970 --> 01:15:26.410
with origin countries, namely a number

01:15:26.410 --> 01:15:31.410
of northern African, some
sub-Saharan countries as well.

01:15:31.850 --> 01:15:34.640
And this is what we'll do in the future.

01:15:34.640 --> 01:15:38.010
To concentrate on that
and the deal is basically

01:15:38.010 --> 01:15:40.560
let's help those countries create the jobs

01:15:40.560 --> 01:15:44.020
for the people who are
looking for jobs in Europe

01:15:45.800 --> 01:15:49.810
and in doing so share with them both

01:15:49.810 --> 01:15:51.870
the responsibility of
addressing the problem

01:15:51.870 --> 01:15:56.760
but also the benefits of economic redress.

01:15:58.620 --> 01:16:03.620
So this is a complex reality
requiring complex solutions.

01:16:04.450 --> 01:16:07.690
On the Brexit question
about free circulation

01:16:07.690 --> 01:16:10.983
I think we are not yet there, right.

01:16:12.910 --> 01:16:14.760
Let me briefly address the Brexit issue

01:16:14.760 --> 01:16:17.450
because I know its important for you.

01:16:17.450 --> 01:16:21.050
First of all, we respect the
choice of the British voters.

01:16:21.050 --> 01:16:24.050
It was a democratic choice, we
have nothing to say about it.

01:16:25.100 --> 01:16:28.850
If you want our opinion,
I think a majority

01:16:28.850 --> 01:16:31.850
of us on the continent
would have preferred

01:16:31.850 --> 01:16:36.700
the British people to stay
in the European Union.

01:16:36.700 --> 01:16:39.690
I certainly do think that
would have been better

01:16:39.690 --> 01:16:41.810
for the European Union and I believe also

01:16:41.810 --> 01:16:43.060
better for the United Kingdom.

01:16:43.060 --> 01:16:45.380
But I am not British, the British have

01:16:45.380 --> 01:16:47.560
the right to decide so full respect for

01:16:47.560 --> 01:16:49.440
the democratic decision.

01:16:49.440 --> 01:16:54.440
Secondly, we are to a large
extent in uncharted waters.

01:16:57.500 --> 01:17:01.060
Never before has a country requested

01:17:01.060 --> 01:17:06.060
to leave the European
Union and we introduced

01:17:06.690 --> 01:17:10.700
the provision in the present
treaty, in the Lisbon Treaty.

01:17:10.700 --> 01:17:13.027
Beautiful city by the
way, that you should--

01:17:13.027 --> 01:17:15.736
(laughing)

01:17:15.736 --> 01:17:17.236
That you should visit one day.

01:17:18.390 --> 01:17:20.290
If you have not yet done so.

01:17:20.290 --> 01:17:22.640
But the Treaty of Lisbon
has for the first time

01:17:22.640 --> 01:17:24.500
in the history of the European Union

01:17:24.500 --> 01:17:26.860
a provision addressing the possibility

01:17:26.860 --> 01:17:29.070
of a country leaving the European Union.

01:17:29.070 --> 01:17:30.963
Which is the famous Article 50.

01:17:31.890 --> 01:17:36.610
And the article basically
says that a country

01:17:36.610 --> 01:17:40.977
that wishes to do so, to
leave the European Union

01:17:40.977 --> 01:17:45.050
has to trigger that article
through formal requests

01:17:45.050 --> 01:17:47.560
for the the starting of negotiations.

01:17:47.560 --> 01:17:50.340
And these negotiations
should take place during

01:17:50.340 --> 01:17:53.310
a period of two years,
after which if there

01:17:53.310 --> 01:17:55.570
is an agreement the
agreement is implemented.

01:17:55.570 --> 01:17:56.930
If there is no agreement the country

01:17:56.930 --> 01:17:58.593
will have to leave anyway.

01:18:00.040 --> 01:18:01.960
This Article 50 has to be triggered

01:18:01.960 --> 01:18:03.840
by the country that wants to leave.

01:18:03.840 --> 01:18:07.103
Not by those who want to stay, obviously.

01:18:08.100 --> 01:18:13.100
And the United Kingdom has not
yet triggered that article.

01:18:14.040 --> 01:18:18.540
And so we are not discussing
any issue formally

01:18:18.540 --> 01:18:22.230
with them until they do so.

01:18:22.230 --> 01:18:24.710
The Prime Minister announced that they

01:18:24.710 --> 01:18:27.530
will do so sometime in March of next year.

01:18:27.530 --> 01:18:29.160
We are waiting for that to happen.

01:18:29.160 --> 01:18:32.773
In the meantime no formal
negotiations take place.

01:18:33.810 --> 01:18:36.450
So its very difficult for
me to anticipate positions

01:18:36.450 --> 01:18:39.500
in that negotiations
but I'm of course aware

01:18:39.500 --> 01:18:41.693
of the debate that is taking place.

01:18:42.770 --> 01:18:45.913
You know we have a set
of rules in our system.

01:18:46.900 --> 01:18:48.960
This set of rules is consistent.

01:18:48.960 --> 01:18:53.960
It is coherent, it encompasses
a number of elements

01:18:56.540 --> 01:19:01.263
of which four fundamental
freedoms are interlinked.

01:19:02.490 --> 01:19:05.673
And including the free
circulation of people.

01:19:06.840 --> 01:19:10.510
So in abstract its very
difficult to imagine

01:19:10.510 --> 01:19:14.010
that we can disentangle
these four freedoms

01:19:14.010 --> 01:19:17.883
in a way that is still
coherent with our treaties.

01:19:18.770 --> 01:19:20.930
But that's as far as I can go now

01:19:20.930 --> 01:19:23.440
because the issue is not yet on the table.

01:19:23.440 --> 01:19:25.930
We don't know yet how
the United Kingdom wants

01:19:25.930 --> 01:19:28.710
to approach it and I
don't want to prejudge

01:19:28.710 --> 01:19:29.733
on their position.

01:19:32.010 --> 01:19:35.180
- [Mary] Okay so we're down
to our last few questions

01:19:35.180 --> 01:19:36.493
and almost outta time.

01:19:38.530 --> 01:19:40.170
Two of the questions want to take up

01:19:40.170 --> 01:19:42.940
the question of the
benign versus darker view

01:19:42.940 --> 01:19:44.013
that you've laid out.

01:19:45.030 --> 01:19:47.563
I'll ask one of these questions first.

01:19:48.630 --> 01:19:51.370
Vladimir Putin seems
like a malevolent force.

01:19:51.370 --> 01:19:53.360
Is there something you
can tell me about him

01:19:53.360 --> 01:19:54.733
that will ease my mind?

01:19:55.660 --> 01:19:56.820
I'm not asking that question.

01:19:56.820 --> 01:19:59.240
Someone else out there is asking.

01:19:59.240 --> 01:20:01.190
But I wouldn't mind easing my mind too.

01:20:03.300 --> 01:20:04.133
- Well.

01:20:06.723 --> 01:20:11.113
I think its up to him to
reply to that question.

01:20:13.540 --> 01:20:14.373
Yeah.

01:20:15.460 --> 01:20:17.270
I think I'll stop there.

01:20:17.270 --> 01:20:19.820
I think I'll stop there, now I mean just

01:20:19.820 --> 01:20:21.210
in the confidence of this room

01:20:21.210 --> 01:20:23.607
which by the way its being streamed so--

01:20:26.519 --> 01:20:29.290
And maybe translated into
Russian, no I mean I--

01:20:33.560 --> 01:20:35.670
The relationship with
Russian for Europeans

01:20:35.670 --> 01:20:40.510
is very important because if there

01:20:40.510 --> 01:20:42.963
is one thing you cannot
change it is geography.

01:20:44.150 --> 01:20:47.250
They will be our neighbors,
we'll be their neighbors.

01:20:47.250 --> 01:20:52.250
And we have thousands of
kilometers of border with Russia

01:20:52.510 --> 01:20:56.970
and we have economies
that are quite interlinked

01:20:56.970 --> 01:20:59.673
and the history, and the
culture, and all that.

01:20:59.673 --> 01:21:04.673
So there is a future
for EU Russia relations.

01:21:05.220 --> 01:21:08.130
We are going through
slightly difficult times

01:21:08.130 --> 01:21:10.053
for reasons you well know.

01:21:11.180 --> 01:21:16.180
But I'm sure we will find ways
to first separate the issues.

01:21:17.600 --> 01:21:20.170
You know, not linking issues

01:21:20.170 --> 01:21:22.093
which are not necessarily linked.

01:21:23.060 --> 01:21:25.640
Stick to our values and our principles

01:21:25.640 --> 01:21:27.930
and state them very clearly.

01:21:27.930 --> 01:21:31.110
But in areas where we
can cooperate well we

01:21:31.110 --> 01:21:32.680
are ready to continue to cooperate.

01:21:32.680 --> 01:21:34.440
Take the Iran deal, for instance.

01:21:34.440 --> 01:21:38.490
Where we, the cooperation between us

01:21:38.490 --> 01:21:41.860
and Russia and the US and China

01:21:41.860 --> 01:21:46.860
was critical to allow the deal
that we reached with Iran.

01:21:49.000 --> 01:21:51.940
And we did this at the same
time where we're having

01:21:51.940 --> 01:21:53.920
very difficult discussions
with the Russians

01:21:53.920 --> 01:21:55.750
about Ukraine and Crimea.

01:21:55.750 --> 01:21:57.340
So I think there's ways and means for us

01:21:57.340 --> 01:22:00.810
to find the balance but
we will never compromise

01:22:00.810 --> 01:22:04.053
on a number of principles
as I said earlier.

01:22:07.480 --> 01:22:08.940
- [Mary] So I think a follow up to that

01:22:08.940 --> 01:22:11.980
is given the benign versus
darker views you spoke about

01:22:11.980 --> 01:22:14.150
how do you view the responsibility

01:22:14.150 --> 01:22:18.890
of the EU to protect these
values that you're speaking

01:22:18.890 --> 01:22:21.770
of so that they may continue to evolve

01:22:21.770 --> 01:22:23.710
and be applied in the future?

01:22:23.710 --> 01:22:26.973
- Yeah I mean if you
want to be credible you

01:22:26.973 --> 01:22:28.403
have to be coherent.

01:22:30.560 --> 01:22:35.170
If you compromise on your
values how can you be

01:22:35.170 --> 01:22:39.213
an advocate for those values
towards the rest of the world?

01:22:40.820 --> 01:22:44.460
Is as simple or as complicated as that.

01:22:44.460 --> 01:22:48.810
And so I think we will
stick to the formula

01:22:48.810 --> 01:22:53.810
that we in order to be coherent we need

01:22:55.610 --> 01:22:58.160
to be credible and vice versa.

01:22:58.160 --> 01:23:01.470
So we'll see as you
continue to us on forefront

01:23:01.470 --> 01:23:05.830
of the fight for human
rights wherever the issue

01:23:07.500 --> 01:23:12.130
or the violation of those
rights takes place you

01:23:12.130 --> 01:23:14.910
will find us as we have,
and we just finished

01:23:14.910 --> 01:23:17.180
some work in the UN on the committee

01:23:17.180 --> 01:23:18.460
that deals with human rights.

01:23:18.460 --> 01:23:21.823
And we were leading the
fight on death penalty.

01:23:22.830 --> 01:23:27.713
On DPRK, on freedom of
religion and belief.

01:23:28.860 --> 01:23:31.980
On a number of areas
in which on the rights

01:23:31.980 --> 01:23:34.833
of the LGBT community.

01:23:35.810 --> 01:23:39.820
All that on the rights of women, children.

01:23:39.820 --> 01:23:42.630
I mean we are on front
front of those rights.

01:23:42.630 --> 01:23:44.880
And for us to be effective there we need

01:23:44.880 --> 01:23:48.090
to be absolutely coherent
on the internal front

01:23:48.090 --> 01:23:49.360
as we are today.

01:23:49.360 --> 01:23:52.120
For instance you cannot be a
member of the European Union

01:23:52.120 --> 01:23:54.803
if you have death penalty in your country.

01:23:56.440 --> 01:23:58.050
As simple as that.

01:23:58.050 --> 01:24:02.893
If you do not uphold fully human rights.

01:24:03.810 --> 01:24:07.733
So these are fundamental
pillars of our construction.

01:24:08.970 --> 01:24:11.030
- [Mary] I think on that strong note

01:24:11.030 --> 01:24:13.200
this is a good place to
end and thank you again

01:24:13.200 --> 01:24:14.400
so much for your time.

01:24:15.296 --> 01:24:17.546
(applause)

01:24:20.068 --> 01:24:21.298
- Thank you.

01:24:21.298 --> 01:24:23.548
(applause)

